nd B7-B8-B9 \u2014 B6 is between them. They might be aiming for a Full Flush in Bams with sequences bridging to B6. Also, they have concealed a kong of B1 and seem active in Bams \u2014 discarding B6 could feed a potential win. High risk.\n\nReason:  \nThe safest discard is **W3** because:\n- All W1, W2, W4 tiles are already accounted for in other players\u2019 melds or discards (each at 4 copies exposed).\n- Therefore, no player can form a Chow including W3 ([W1-W2-W3], [W2-W3-W4], [W3-W4-W5]) anymore.\n- W3 cannot be used for a Pung (all four W3s would need to exist \u2014 only three remain unseen, and one is in my hand; others haven\u2019t shown W3, so unlikely any player has two).\n- Thus, W3 is **safe to discard** with near-zero risk.\nOther options like B9 and B6 are dangerous due to opponents\u2019 visible Bams sequences.\n\nAnswer: Play W3"}
